.TH SENTENCES 1
.SH NAME
sentences \- reflow column-wrapped prose into one sentence per line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sentences
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Sentences
reads
.I file
(standard input if none given) and writes it back out with exactly one
sentence per line, regardless of how the input's own lines were broken.
A blank line still ends a paragraph, but every other line between two
blank lines is joined into one run of text first, with the wraps that
wanted the line to fit a fixed column removed, and only then cut again -
at a
.BR . ,
.B !
or
.B ?
followed by white space or the end of the paragraph, not at wherever the
original line happened to end.
.PP
This exists for
.IR speak (1),
which reads one utterance per input line - the right thing for
.B ls /dev
or a directory listing, one item to a line, and the wrong thing for
prose that
.IR man2txt (1)
or any other formatter has wrapped at 65 columns with no regard for
where a sentence ends. Piped straight through
.IR speak ,
a wrapped man page pauses in the middle of a sentence and runs straight
through the period at the end of the next one; piped through this
first, it pauses where a person reading it aloud would:
.IP
.EX
man2txt /man/1/paste | sentences | speak
.EE
.IP
For a man page specifically,
.IR man2speak (1)
is better than
.I man2txt
here: it keeps section headings out of the paragraphs that follow them,
which this program - having only blank lines to go on - cannot recover
once they're gone.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sentences.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR speak (1),
.IR man2txt (1),
.IR man2speak (1)
.SH BUGS
Sentence boundaries are detected by punctuation alone: an abbreviation
like
.B e.g.
or
.B Mr.
ends a sentence early, the same wrong way any rule this simple does.
Fixing that needs real abbreviation knowledge, which this does not have.
.PP
A block of code or shell commands inside a man page's
.B EXAMPLE
section has no sentence-ending punctuation of its own, so it is joined
into one long run rather than split sensibly - this reflows prose, and a
shell pipeline is not prose. Reading an
.B EXAMPLE
section understandably is a different problem, not attempted here.
